From 44fd9380c710feb9b4a55da63eb8571e1fc5c5a4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xFFshell <0xffshell@users.noreply.gitlab.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 12:34:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Point the stare at whoever hit us, not past them

A flinch is measured against the head it is added to, and on the walking path
that head has the walk's glance on it: base + walkGlance + sway + flinch. The
stare was advanced against `base` alone, so a blow that landed while the road
was being looked at left the head on the attacker plus the glance - up to seventy
degrees of cow, for as long as the glance had left to run, which is the whole of
what this behaviour exists to fix. The base handed to Flinch#advance is now the
head it is added to, the glance included, and Flinch says so where its base is
documented. The sway is deliberately still not in there: the sway is meant to
keep riding on the stare.

And while a blow is still owed a look the glances stand down - the walking one on
its own gate, the idle one on the caller's - because a person staring at whoever
just hit them is not also looking at the cow, and the stare is measured against
the head a glance would be putting on. That is also what makes the first fix
visible at the end of an episode: the head comes home to the aim rather than
swinging back out to finish a glance that was interrupted by being punched.

`HumanlikeLookFuzzTest` carries the walk's glance now, and the caller's sums:
the glance is advanced on the caller's own gates - welcome on a plain walk, the
target, the click, the stare, and the executor's out-clause once the feet have
stopped for a look - and put on the head in the caller's order, with the stare
measured against the head plus it. The six offsets that land on one head now
cannot jump further in a tick than the hand's flick plus a glance plus a flinch,
and the neck's bound is held from tick to tick on walking heads. Modelling the
glance is what turned the run's blow rate down: at four blows in a hundred an
answering flinch is the standing state, and a glance - which needs a hundred to
three hundred welcome ticks - never gets to run at all. Three in a thousand is a
punch every sixteen seconds, and both machines then get the run they need.

The one rule a hostile run of drawn aims cannot judge is where a stare ends up,
because a stare is a chase there; that is a focused test now - aStareLandsOnWho-
everHitUsWithAGlanceOut, the aim held still, the glance out at forty degrees -
which is the walking-path defect above, written down so it cannot come back.

Validation: ./gradlew :test green, 304 tests, 0 failures, 0 skipped.
